 (notes
  ("E" . "E, makind the first sillab alone is a altin preposition, as edict, euent. The affinitie between, e, and ye quik i, causeth a duble serch in the table, intent, or entent")
  ("G" . "The strong g, before, e, and i, in English is warranted by the like in the greke tung")
  ("H" . "H, is so gentlie pronounced, or rather so not pnounced in our tung, as manie words, which begin with it, maie be sought for by ther first vowell, rather then h, onelesse the originall be well known, as honest, humble, honor, hostage, &c. which sound vpon the, o, not aspirate")
  ("I" . "The frst person alwaie great")
  ("I" . "There is great kindred betwen the weak, g, & i, the consonant & therefor theie enterchange oftimes both sound & place, & so in the table maie cause searching in both the letters")
 ))
